 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery.** Five-second HTTPS polling would be simpler to operate statelessly but adds repeated reads and several seconds of lag; a hosted realtime service would reduce connection operations but introduce recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted sockets provide fast updates without vendor dependency, at the accepted cost of owning connection capacity, reliable publication, replayable revisions, reconnects, and browser catch-up.
